Step 1: Pull the Official Nagios Docker Image

👉 Use the most common Nagios image: jasonrivers/nagios

Command
docker pull jasonrivers/nagios:latest

Expected Output
latest: Pulling from jasonrivers/nagios
Digest: sha256:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
Status: Downloaded newer image for jasonrivers/nagios:latest
docker.io/jasonrivers/nagios:latest

✅ Step 2: Run Nagios Container (Expose Ports)

Nagios typically uses:

Port 80 → Web UI

Volume mounts → for configs (optional)

Command
docker run --name nagios \
 -p 8080:80 \
 -d jasonrivers/nagios:latest

Expected Output
<container_id>


Check container is running:

docker ps


Expected:

CONTAINER ID   IMAGE                     PORTS                 NAMES
abc123xyz      jasonrivers/nagios        0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp  nagios

✅ Step 3: Access Nagios Web Interface
URL
http://localhost:8080/nagios

Default Login (for this image)

Username: nagiosadmin

Password: nagios

✅ Step 4: Show CPU, Memory & Service Uptime Monitoring

Once logged in:

✔ Go to "Host Status"
✔ Go to "Service Detail"
✔ You will see:

CPU Load

Memory Usage

Swap Usage

Uptime

HTTP / SSH checks

